arXiv:2603.13384v1 [cs.SE] 11 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">VulnAgent-X: A Layered Agentic Framework for Repository-Level Vulnerability Detection</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Renwei Meng<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Email: <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">R32314095@stu.ahu.edu.cn</span></span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoyi Wu<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jingming Wang<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoyan Bai
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Software vulnerability detection is critical in software engineering as security flaws arise from complex interactions across code structure, repository context, and runtime conditions. Existing methods are limited by local code views, one-shot prediction, and insufficient validation, reducing reliability in realistic repository-level settings. This study proposes VulnAgentX, a layered agentic framework integrating lightweight risk screening, bounded context expansion, specialised analysis agents, selective dynamic verification, and evidence fusion into a unified pipeline. Experiments on function-level and just-in-time vulnerability benchmarks show VulnAgent-X outperforms static baselines, encoder-based models, and simpler agentic variants, with better localisation and balanced performance-cost trade-offs. Treating vulnerability detection as a staged, evidence-driven auditing process improves detection quality, reduces false positives, and produces interpretable results for repository-level software security analysis. Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/xiaolu-666113/Vlun-Agent-X" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/xiaolu-666113/Vlun-Agent-X</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>vulnerability detection software security code intelligence large language models software agents.
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>These authors contributed equally.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Software vulnerabilities are hard to detect due to long-range program dependencies, repository context, and runtime conditions. Classical learning-based methods (VulDeePecker, SySeVR, Devign) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> learn vulnerability patterns but struggle with cross-file/context-dependent defects as they rely on localised code units.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While large language models (CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> enhanced code understanding, stronger representations do not guarantee better security reasoning—AI-assisted code generation still introduces insecure implementations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent agent-based workflows (SWE-bench, ReAct, Reflexion, SWE-agent, AutoCodeRover) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> improve repository-scale reasoning, and PrimeVul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> revealed older benchmarks overestimate model capability in realistic settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This study frames vulnerability detection as an evidence-driven, staged process (triage, retrieval, analysis, verification, adjudication) instead of one-shot prediction, ensuring decisions are evidence-supported rather than isolated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Learning-based vulnerability detection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early neural vulnerability detectors validated data-driven security analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: VulDeePecker proposed code gadgets for classification, SySeVR extended vulnerability representation via syntax/semantic views, and Devign leveraged graph neural models for richer program structure. While foundational, these methods rely on fixed local inputs and single-pass prediction, limiting support for repository-level interactions and evolving evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Code language models and review-oriented code intelligence</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">General-purpose code models (CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> enhanced code representation via large-scale pre-training, and CodeReviewer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> showed pre-training on code changes/artefacts boosts review-oriented tasks. While providing strong semantic priors, these studies are mostly predictive (not interactive) and lack explicit evidence gathering, hypothesis testing, or signal reconciliation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Agentic software engineering</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic methods (ReAct, Reflexion) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> improved tough tasks via alternating reasoning and actions/feedback. In SE, SWE-bench, SWE-agent, AutoCodeRover <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> showed value in repository navigation, search and refinement. Yet these systems focus on issue resolution/maintenance, not security-first vulnerability discovery (critical for false negatives/positives and evidence quality).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Gap addressed by this study</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work integrates ideas from vulnerability detection, code language modelling and agentic SE, focusing on security auditing. Its key distinction: framing vulnerability detection as a multi-stage evidence accumulation process (with counter-evidence/selective verification), not static classification or generic repository problem-solving.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VulnAgent-X transforms vulnerability detection into a staged inference process: identify suspicious regions, retrieve bounded repository context, invoke specialised agents, validate high-risk findings, and fuse evidence into a final decision. This design improves accuracy, interpretability, and efficiency under realistic conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.13384v1/images/figure1.jpg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/258;" width="476" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Agent Workflow</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Task formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> denote a repository and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denote the analysis target (e.g., function, code region, diff hunk). The output is a set of findings:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(x,R)=\left\{f_{1},\ldots,f_{N}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(x,R)=\left\{f_{1},\ldots,f_{N}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each finding <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}=\left(t_{i},l_{i},e_{i},c_{i},s_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}=\left(t_{i},l_{i},e_{i},c_{i},s_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> (issue type <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, location <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, evidence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, confidence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, severity <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Stage I: Fast risk screening</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system performs low-cost triage to identify suspicious regions. Each candidate region receives an initial score:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Risk}_{0}\left(r_{i}\right)=\alpha S_{i}^{\text{stat }}+\beta S_{i}^{\text{meta }}+\gamma S_{i}^{\text{lm }}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Risk</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>stat </mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>meta </mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>lm </mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Risk}_{0}\left(r_{i}\right)=\alpha S_{i}^{\text{stat }}+\beta S_{i}^{\text{meta }}+\gamma S_{i}^{\text{lm }}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Only top-<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> regions are retained for deeper analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Stage II: Context expansion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Context Agent builds a compact context set for each selected region (callers/callees, config files, tests, similar changes), ranked by relevance:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Rel}\left(c,r_{i}\right)=\lambda_{1}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{sem}}+\lambda_{2}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{dep}}+\lambda_{3}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{chg}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Rel</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Sim</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Sim</mi><mi>dep</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Sim</mi><mi>chg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Rel}\left(c,r_{i}\right)=\lambda_{1}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{sem}}+\lambda_{2}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{dep}}+\lambda_{3}\operatorname{Sim}_{\mathrm{chg}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Only highest-ranked elements are retained to ensure bounded yet useful context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Stage III: Multi-agent analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework launches specialised agents:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Router Agent: predicts issue type and dispatches downstream experts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic Analysis Agent: inspects structural edits, control flow, boundary checks, exception logic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Security Analysis Agent: analyses source-to-sink flows, missing sanitisation, access control, weakness patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Logic Bug Agent: focuses on non-security correctness issues (ordering errors, invalid state transitions, race-prone updates).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sceptic Agent: searches for counter-evidence refuting weak/unreachable claims.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This decomposition enables complementary code inspection instead of monolithic judgement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Stage IV: Dynamic verification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For high-risk/uncertain findings, a Verification Agent generates minimal validation artefacts (reproducing inputs, unit tests, payloads). This step is selective to balance reliability and runtime cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Stage V: Evidence fusion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Final decisions combine static signals, contextual evidence, agent agreement, dynamic verification, and counter-evidence:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Score}\left(f_{i}\right)=w_{1}\operatorname{Risk}_{0}+w_{2}E_{i}^{\mathrm{stat}}+w_{3}E_{i}^{\mathrm{ctx}}+w_{4}E_{i}^{\mathrm{agt}}+w_{5}E_{i}^{\mathrm{dyn}}-w_{6}E_{i}^{\mathrm{ctr}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Score</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Risk</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>stat</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ctx</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>agt</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ctr</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Score}\left(f_{i}\right)=w_{1}\operatorname{Risk}_{0}+w_{2}E_{i}^{\mathrm{stat}}+w_{3}E_{i}^{\mathrm{ctx}}+w_{4}E_{i}^{\mathrm{agt}}+w_{5}E_{i}^{\mathrm{dyn}}-w_{6}E_{i}^{\mathrm{ctr}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Output includes issue type, localisation, evidence summary, confidence, and remediation guidance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.8 </span>Confidence-aware escalation and memory</h3>

<div id="S3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework uses threshold-based escalation (easy cases exit early, moderate cases get extra review, high-severity/low-confidence cases trigger verification) and maintains session/repository memory for consistency and project-specific knowledge.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Design and Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental objectives</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments address these research questions:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ1: Does VulnAgent-X outperform static/neural/agentic baselines on function/commit-level detection?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ2: Does the framework improve fine-grained localisation quality?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ3: Which components contribute most to performance?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ4: How sensitive is the framework to key hyperparameters (suspicious-region/context budget, confidence thresholds)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ5: What is the performance-cost trade-off of layered escalation?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ6: What error patterns reveal remaining limitations?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Core experiments use four benchmarks aligned with realistic security practice:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Devign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: Classical function-level benchmark for cross-study comparison.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Big-Vul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>: Large C/C++ dataset (3,754 vulnerabilities, 348 GitHub projects, 91 types).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PrimeVul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>: Main function-level benchmark (reduced duplication, reliable labels, chronological splits).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">JULY <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>: Commit-level benchmark (20,274 commits from 506 C/C++ projects) for triage/localisation testing.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Evaluation protocol: (1) preserve official splits; (2) 80/10/10 split for Devign/Big-Vul (no official split); (3) cross-project split for robustness; (4) official chronological split for PrimeVul.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VulnAgent-X is compared against five baseline groups:</p>
<ol id="S4.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Static baseline: Lightweight rule-based security screening.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural baselines: Devign-style GNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, fine-tuned CodeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, fine-tuned GraphCodeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single-agent LLM: Direct vulnerability judgement (no collaboration).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RAG-only baseline: LLM + retrieved context (no specialised agents).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential multi-stage baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>: Modern agentic competitor.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Implementation protocol</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All LLM-based variants use the same code-oriented decoder model (differences in workflow, not model scale) and context/decoding configuration. Default VulnAgent-X settings:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">suspicious-region budget <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">context budget <math id="S4.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=6,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=6,000</annotation></semantics></math> tokens;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">early-acceptance threshold <math id="S4.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{high }}=0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>high </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{high }}=0.82</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">escalation threshold <math id="S4.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{low }}=0.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>low </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{low }}=0.48</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">dynamic verification for high-severity/low-confidence cases only.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Evaluation metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation uses three metric categories:</p>
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Detection: Precision, recall, F1-score, AUROC (binary detection).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Localisation: Top-1/Top-3 accuracy, MRR (suspicious statements/changed lines).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficiency: Average prompt tokens/time per sample, early-exit rate.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Main results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS6.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Function-level detection</h4>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Function-level detection ‣ 4.6 Main results ‣ 4 Experimental Design and Results ‣ VulnAgent-X: A Layered Agentic Framework for Repository-Level Vulnerability Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows VulnAgent-X achieves the highest F1/AUROC on all benchmarks, with largest gains on PrimeVul (contextual reasoning/false-positive control most valuable). All methods perform worse on PrimeVul (more realistic setting).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Function-level vulnerability detection results.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Devign</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Big-Vul</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">PrimeVul</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">F1</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AUROC</span></th>
