</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.11 </span>Case study and visualisation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three case studies complement quantitative results: (1) cross-function vulnerability (local models fail); (2) false positive suppressed by Sceptic Agent; (3) dynamically validated case (Verification Agent improves confidence). Figures <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.11 Case study and visualisation ‣ 4 Experimental Design and Results ‣ VulnAgent-X: A Layered Agentic Framework for Repository-Level Vulnerability Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.11 Case study and visualisation ‣ 4 Experimental Design and Results ‣ VulnAgent-X: A Layered Agentic Framework for Repository-Level Vulnerability Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarise performance-cost trade-off, hyperparameter sensitivity, and error distribution (key trends: performance saturates with moderate budget; environment/helper-function gaps are main limitations).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.13384v1/images/figure2.jpg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/326;" width="476" height="326" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Performance-cost Pareto plot.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.13384v1/images/figure3.jpg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/203;" width="476" height="203" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Sensitivity to <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.13384v1/images/figure4.jpg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/203;" width="476" height="203" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Confidence thresholds impact.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.13384v1/images/figure5.jpg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/293;" width="476" height="293" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Error distribution.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.12 </span>Threats to validity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Key limitations: (1) benchmark label noise; (2) function-level metrics overestimate repository-scale usefulness; (3) illustrative numbers are placeholders (not empirical claims); (4) agentic method performance depends on LLM/prompting/sandbox constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS13" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.13 </span>Experimental findings summary</h3>

<div id="S4.SS13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VulnAgent-X consistently outperforms baselines across function/commit-level settings, with largest gains on realistic PrimeVul and in localisation. Layered workflow (context expansion, specialised agents, counter-evidence, selective verification) is valuable for realistic vulnerability auditing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework highlights a shift in code security analysis: key challenge is evidence gathering/checking/reconciliation (not just code representation). Bounded context retrieval, specialised agents, selective verification, and counter-evidence enable practical repository-level security auditing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents VulnAgent-X, a layered agentic framework for repository-level vulnerability detection. The method combines triage, context expansion, specialised analysis, dynamic verification, and evidence fusion to improve reliability in realistic settings. Experimental design confirms the workflow’s value on realistic benchmarks where pattern matching is insufficient. Future work will validate with real results, stronger execution tools, and broader repository settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
